-- What is not an origin label, now that `src'` is one token - `Token::OriginLabel`.
--
-- The lexer fuses a name and the marker only when the name is unqualified, lowercase and written
-- directly against it. That is not a restriction added on top: it is what a label *is*, and having
-- the lexer say so is what let the type parser stop deciding it. `parseLoanLabel` had been saving
-- and restoring the lexer to find out, and the branch reading `v'a` was copied into `parseType` and
-- `parseAType` because both had to make the same decision from the same two tokens.

data Cell {v: Int}

-- A space. The marker is part of the name rather than a token that happens to follow it.
fn spaced(c: src 'Cell) -> Int = c.v

-- Capitalized. A group is named the way a type variable is, and for the same reason: it is bound by
-- the declaration rather than declared somewhere a reader can look it up.
fn capitalized(c: Src'Cell) -> Int = c.v

-- Qualified, and this is the one that used to be *accepted*: `Core.thing'Cell` resolved as a group
-- named `Core.thing`. A group is numbered per declaration, so there is no name outside one for it to
-- be - which makes a qualified label a group nothing can be in.
fn qualified(c: Core.thing'Cell) -> Int = c.v

-- `yield` with no marker, the other half of the same rule, is `Reject.OriginLabel.Yield.yana`.

fn main() -> Int = 0
